    "taxGuide.norway.source.goodsConditions": "Official authority source for goods, luggage, unused-goods and exclusion conditions. Norway is not an EU procedure. The traveller must be resident abroad and agree refund eligibility with the retailer because Norwegian Customs does not pay refunds. Official thresholds and proof routes depend on residence: residents of Sweden, Denmark and Finland use an import-VAT proof procedure, other residents use an invoice-based route, and Svalbard and Jan Mayen have separate treatment. Route one uses retailer form RD-0032 E, personal presentation of form and goods at a staffed customs location within one month after delivery, no retrospective attestation after departure, and retailer payment after the attested form is returned. Route two uses a private Tax-Free Shopping undertaking document that must not be stamped by Norwegian Customs; operator conditions and fees may apply.",
    "taxGuide.norway.source.vatRate": "Official tax source for the standard rate used by shared rule data. Norway is not an EU procedure. The traveller must be resident abroad and agree refund eligibility with the retailer because Norwegian Customs does not pay refunds. Official thresholds and proof routes depend on residence: residents of Sweden, Denmark and Finland use an import-VAT proof procedure, other residents use an invoice-based route, and Svalbard and Jan Mayen have separate treatment. Route one uses retailer form RD-0032 E, personal presentation of form and goods at a staffed customs location within one month after delivery, no retrospective attestation after departure, and retailer payment after the attested form is returned. Route two uses a private Tax-Free Shopping undertaking document that must not be stamped by Norwegian Customs; operator conditions and fees may apply.",
    "taxGuide.ireland.eligibilitySummary": "Traveller eligibility. The Retail Export Scheme is for tourists or travellers resident outside the EU; Northern Ireland residents are excluded and Great Britain residents may qualify with current proof. The retailer charges tax, issues an export voucher, obtains residence evidence, inbound and outbound travel dates and a signed traveller declaration, and refunds directly or through a refund agent after proof of export. Goods are for private use, exported in personal luggage by the last day of the third month after supply, kept available for Customs inspection, and services are excluded. Ordinary non-high-value goods may receive Irish certification even when Ireland is not the final EU departure if official ticket and deadline conditions are met. High-value goods require direct Customs Officer presentation; drop boxes or refund-agent submission are insufficient, with final-EU-exit and short-transit rules applied as Revenue describes.",
